<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oda Nobutada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1557">1557</a>–<a href="/years/1582">1582</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oda Nobutada was the eldest son and heir of the Japanese warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. He received headship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda clan</a> in the mid-1570s and led the campaign that destroyed the Takeda in 1582. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a> attacked Nobunaga at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honnō-ji</a> in June 1582, Nobutada was trapped at Nijō in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and took his own life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobutada.jpg" alt="Oda Nobutada" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 投稿者がファイル作成 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oda_Nobutada3_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Oda Nobutada was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a> around 1557. His childhood name was Kimyo-Maru, and he first called himself Oda Kankuro Nobushige before taking the name Nobutada. His nurse was a daughter of Takigawa Kazumasu, one of his father's senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and there is a theory that he was adopted by Nohime. To strengthen an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda clan</a>, an engagement was arranged between him and Matsuhime, sixth daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>, but it lapsed when Shingen invaded Oda territory in 1572 and then died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Nobutada fought in the Ishiyama Hongan-ji War, at the Siege of Iwamura Castle in 1574 and in the attack on Ise Nagashima. In 1575, as supreme commander at Iwamura, he beat off a night attack by the Takeda and took the castle from their commander Akiyama Nobutomo. In 1577 he led the campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsunaga-hisahide">Matsunaga Hisahide</a>, with generals such as Hosokawa Fujitaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a> under him, and took Shigisan Castle, where Hisahide and his son had shut themselves in. From about then he commanded the Oda generals in his father's place, and he was raised to the third court rank.</p><p>In 1578 he led the army sent to relieve Kōzuki Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harima-province">Harima Province</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mori Terumoto</a>, though Nobunaga ordered a withdrawal and the castle fell. He also took part in suppressing the rebellion of Araki Murashige at Itami, and in 1580 the banishment of Sakuma Nobumori and Ando Morinari widened his lands in Mino and Owari. In 1582 he led 50,000 troops of Mino and Owari into Kai Province with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujimasa">Hojo Ujimasa</a>. He took Ida and Takato castles, leading the assault on Takato himself, and pursued Takeda Katsuyori, who killed himself with his son Nobuyuki after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tenmokuzan">Battle of Tenmokuzan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Nobunaga entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kofu">Kofu</a> on 26 March 1582 he praised his son's service, gave him a sword and spoke of handing on to him rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda clan</a> and the realm. Nobutada died on 21 June 1582, quartered near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honnō-ji</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a>'s men attacked his quarters and he committed seppuku. His sons included his eldest, Oda Hidenobu, and Oda Hidenori.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oda Nobutada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1582 his general Akechi Mitsuhide attacked him at the Honno-ji temple in Kyoto, where Nobunaga died; his heir <b>Nobutada</b> was also killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/oda-regime">Oda regime</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in New Nijō Palace. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.12,137.04&amp;year=1573" aria-label="Owari Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8021 893 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Oda Nobutada"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8471 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8471 1163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.12,137.04&amp;year=1573">What was here in 1573? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
